\right)$ in cylindrical coordinates to rectangular coordinates.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6af68aee1eaad2b0c15d209d99f253bede6ebe4ce628477c180b2d5711c8bdbd"},"seconds":12.990797996520996,"seed":494974159182226851,"stop":"eos","text":"To convert the point \\((6\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}, -2)\\) from cylindrical coordinates to rectangular coordinates, we use the following relationships between cylindrical coordinates \\((r, \\theta, z)\\) and rectangular coordinates \\((x, y, z)\\):\n\n\\[\nx = r \\cos \\theta\n\\]\n\\[\ny = r \\sin \\theta\n\\]\n\\[\nz = z\n\\]\n\nGiven the cylindrical coordinates \\((r, \\theta, z) = (6\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}, -2)\\), we can substitute these values into the equations above.\n\nFirst, calculate \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = r \\cos \\theta = 6\\sqrt{3} \\cos \\left( \\frac{5\\pi}{3} \\right)\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\cos \\left( \\frac{5\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\cos \\left( 2\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\cos \\left( -\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\cos \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2}\\). Therefore,\n\n\\[\nx = 6\\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = 3\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\ny = r \\sin \\theta = 6\\sqrt{3} \\sin \\left( \\frac{5\\pi}{3} \\right)\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\sin \\left( \\frac{5\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\sin \\left( 2\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\sin \\left( -\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) = -\\sin \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). Therefore,\n\n\\[\ny = 6\\sqrt{3} \\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 6 \\cdot -\\frac{3}{2} = -9\n\\]\n\nFinally, the \\(z\\)-coordinate remains the same:\n\n\\[\nz = -2\n\\]\n\nThus, the rectangular coordinates are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(3\\sqrt{3}, -9, -2)}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(3\\sqrt{3}, -9, -2)}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:430"}
